  "query_preview": "..\n\nHeat and humidity have returned today with most of the area now \nseeing temps in the upper 80s and dewpoints in the upper 60s. Model \nadvertised elevated warm layer was sampled by the 12Z KDTX RAOB at \n725mb providing a modest cap to convective potential this evening. \nWhile CAMs suggest around 1000 J/kg of both SB/MLCAPE available late \nday, they struggle to overcome said cap and generate much more than \nan isolated pop-up shower/storm. Afternoon radar trends appear to \nconfirm this with only isolated pop-up light showers thus far. As \nsuch, its more likely that the vast majority of the area stays dry \nthis evening. The conditional severe chance if any updraft can break \nthrough the warm layer is likewise low this evening as column winds \nbelow 700mb hold aob 20kts confining the bulk of shear above 3km. \nFor the late evening-overnight period, there has been notable \nconvergence amongst high-res models in the CAPE gradient failing to \ncross Lake Michigan, instead stalling along the WI shoreline. This \nresults in developing MCS(s) over the western Lake Superior basin to \nexhibit a due south drift in track along Lake Michigan entirely \nmissing SE MI (aside from perhaps some light trailing stratiform \nshowers clipping portions CWA). While the forecast tonight will \nstill keep a slight chance (15-20%) mention for rain, severe chances \nare now looking to be minimal.\n\nHow exactly Monday panes out will depend on the progression of \ntonight's storms over the western Great Lakes, in large part their \naffects on the CAPE gradient. With the current favored scenario of a \nLake Michigan track, this effectively pinches off southern lower MI \ninstability confining main reservoir to WI/IL Monday. With weak \nlower level winds still in place, advection of this gradient east is \nslow, not reaching central southern MI until Monday late afternoon \npushing the main local convective window back towards ~22-00Z. Still \na good spread in outcomes wrt to the robustness of said convection \nhowever given the stronger synoptic setup (ie mid-level trough \ndigging into the central Great Lakes and an associated surface cold \nfront dropping south) would expect at least scattered thunderstorm \ncoverage locally. For severe potential, forecast soundings show only \nmarginal improvements in low level winds relative to today (~5kt \nincrease) again confining the majority of shear around/above 3km. \nMagnitude of available instability is still uncertain given the \ndependency of advection, but shear profile does not lend confidence \nin severe storm chances.",
